`onOperations` receives a flat list that can be arbitrarily long — a backfill over a drive's full history arrives page by page, not operation by operation. Writing one row per operation turns that into one database round-trip per operation. Both shipped analytics processors use the same buffer-and-chunk shape, and the migration guide names it as the recommended pattern:

```typescript
const CHUNK_SIZE = 50;
const buffer: AnalyticsSeriesInput[] = [];

for (const { operation, context } of operations) {
  // ...build seriesInput...
  buffer.push(seriesInput);

  while (buffer.length >= CHUNK_SIZE) {
    const batch = buffer.splice(0, CHUNK_SIZE);
    await this.analyticsStore.addSeriesValues(batch);
  }
}

if (buffer.length > 0) {
  await this.analyticsStore.addSeriesValues(buffer);
}
```

Two details are easy to get wrong. The flush uses `while`, not `if` — with `splice` draining the front, `while` is correct for a buffer that could already be over the threshold, and it keeps the invariant "never hold more than CHUNK_SIZE-1 items". And the trailing `if (buffer.length > 0)` is not optional: without it every partial final chunk is silently dropped.

`IAnalyticsStore` exposes both `addSeriesValue(input)` and `addSeriesValues(inputs)`. The chunked pattern uses the plural form; the singular is a convenience for one-off writes and is what the older Analytics Engine prose shows.

Because a processor registered with `startFrom: "beginning"` can be re-delivered its entire history — on first registration against a drive with history, after a restart gap, or after `tracked.retry()` — chunked inserts alone are not idempotent. The `clearSource` companion pattern handles that: on seeing operation index 0 again for a known document, call `clearSeriesBySource(source, true)` and rebuild, so reprocessing converges on the same totals.

Provenance: academy `04-Reference/04-Processors/08-ProcessorMigrationGuide.md` (published) and the draft `03-Build/04-WorkWithData/_04-analytics-processor.md`; code verified byte-for-byte in `packages/shared/processors/drive-analytics/{drive,document}-processor.ts` at 6.2.2-dev.45.
